The origins of the press are rooted in the 15th century, specifically in the workshop of Johannes Gutenberg. While the exact date of his invention is debated, the technology he developed—using movable type and a screw press—was a radical departure from the traditional methods of copying manuscripts. Prior to Gutenberg, books were produced by hand, a labor-intensive process that resulted in thousands of variations in every single copy. Gutenberg's innovation allowed for the mass production of identical texts, a concept that would later be termed "standardization." This shift was not merely about efficiency; it was about consistency. A book printed by Gutenberg could be sold to a thousand people, whereas a manuscript copied by hand could only be sold to a handful of buyers.

The immediate impact of this technology was profound, particularly in the realm of religion. The invention of movable type allowed for the rapid dissemination of religious texts, such as the Bible, across Europe. This democratization of scripture led to the Reformation, a period of intense religious and political conflict between the Catholic Church and Protestant groups. The ability to mass-produce and distribute religious literature allowed reformers to spread their ideas rapidly, challenging the authority of the Church and fostering a sense of national identity among various European nations. The printing press thus became a tool for social and political change, accelerating the Reformation and the rise of nation-states.

